	abstract = {Pallard et al. (Archives of Neurology 1983; 40: 548–51) reported a stroke patient who could not (consciously) detect tactile stiulied applied to her deafferentated right upper limb but could nevertheless localize them by pointing (blindtouch). We now describe a stroke patient who demonstrates the converse dissociation: relatively intact cutaneous detection with frequent failure to show any ability to localize the stimulus. This case and that reported by Paillard et al. accordingly provide evidence for the double dissociation of detection and localization of light pressure.},
